cellSaveData: dirList follows CellSaveDataSetList sortType / sortOrder (newest first for MODIFIEDTIME/DESCENT); sortType or sortOrder above 1 is CELL_SAVEDATA_ERROR_PARAM.

Idea from Re-TeRo17/ps3recomp 480eb2ee libs/system/cellSaveData.c (MIT): their "gp101" change picks the newest save
(savedata_dir_mtime / savedata_newest_fixed) because with several saves the load followed name order. Hand rewrite,
nothing copied; this patch fixes the listing order the firmware gives instead of adding a pick heuristic.

Problem: ListSave2 / ListLoad2 and savedata_fixed (FixedSave2/FixedLoad2, ListAutoSave/ListAutoLoad and the User*
forms) never read sortType (+0) or sortOrder (+4) of the guest CellSaveDataSetList; enumerate_save_dirs filled dirList
in readdir / FindFirstFile order and cut it to dirListMax while reading. A title that takes its first dirList entry,
copies the list into its fixedList, or lets funcList pick nothing (ListLoad2 then falls back to dirList[0]) loaded the
first save by name, not the newest (tod-recomp fixes.md row 13). With dirListMax below the save count the newest save
could be left out of the list altogether.

Fix (libs/system/cellSaveData.c):
- enumerate_save_dirs(prefix, dirList, max, sort_type, sort_order) collects every matching directory, sorts it
  (savedata_sort_dirs) and then copies the first max entries; the return value is still the total (dirNum).
- Keys: MODIFIEDTIME = time, then SUB_TITLE (PARAM.SFO, read_param_sfo); SUBTITLE = SUB_TITLE, then time; the
  directory name last, so the order never depends on the host. DESCENT reverses the whole order. SUB_TITLE compares
  as bytes (unsigned char). One log line per sorted listing: "[cellSaveData] dirList sorted by mtime descent (N dirs):
  first='...'".
- savedata_setlist_sort_ok: sortType > 1 or sortOrder > 1 returns CELL_SAVEDATA_ERROR_PARAM before any callback, in
  ListSave2, ListLoad2 and savedata_fixed.
Behaviour reference: RPCS3 cellSaveData.cpp savedata_op (read 2026-11-19 from GitHub raw, nothing copied): parameter
errors 12/13 for sortType/sortOrder above SUBTITLE/ASCENT; MODIFIEDTIME ties fall back to SUB_TITLE, SUBTITLE ties to
the mtime; ASCENT sorts up, DESCENT is the reverse.

ASSUMPTION: the firmware sorts every matching directory before it cuts the list to dirListMax (RPCS3 cuts first, in
host directory order, then sorts the kept entries).
ASSUMPTION: the time key is the newest of the directory's own mtime, its PARAM.SFO and its files' mtimes. On the PS3
(and in RPCS3, which writes a save into a fresh directory and renames it) the directory mtime is the last save; this
backend rewrites the files in place, so the directory mtime stays at the save's creation. statGet.dir.st_mtime is
unchanged (still the directory's own mtime). Windows reads the file times with FindFirstFile (enumerate_save_files
leaves st_mtime 0 there).
Not changed: the '|'-separated dirNamePrefix list RPCS3 accepts; listParam in dirList stays empty.

Tests: libs/system/tests/test_savedata_sort.c (tools/test_savedata_sort.py; R&C runner with the negative control:
port/rc/tests/run_savedata_sort_test.sh): five saves BCUS00000A..E whose name,
time and SUB_TITLE orders all differ (C and D share a time; E has an old directory and PARAM.SFO but the newest data
file) plus one outside the prefix: dirList for MODIFIEDTIME DESCENT [E B C D A], ASCENT [A D C B E], SUBTITLE ASCENT
[B D E A C], DESCENT [C A E D B]; dirListMax 2 keeps [E B] with dirNum 5; ListLoad2 whose funcList picks nothing loads
E; sortType 2 / sortOrder 2 are CELL_SAVEDATA_ERROR_PARAM. PASS. Negative control (--against a tree without this
patch): FAIL, 8 failures (every listing [A B C D E], the fallback loads A, both bad values return CELL_OK).
test_savedata_root.c calls enumerate_save_dirs with the new arguments (0, 0); it, test_savedata_io,
test_savedata_param_sfo and test_savedata_user_autosave pass before and after; test_savedata_fixed.c fails the same way
before and after (its assert vm_read32(cb) == 0 predates the 7102 sentinel).

